issues.body:
### Preflight Checklist

- [x] I have searched existing issues. Related but different: #88548 (the same loop skips issues because labeling reorders the list it pages through) and #87647 (issues labeled "has repro" closed by the sweep).
- [x] This is a single bug report

This is about this repo's own automation (`scripts/sweep.ts`), not the Claude Code CLI, so the version, platform and terminal fields don't apply.

### What's Wrong?

`markStale` lists open issues oldest-updated first, 10 pages of 100, and skips issues that are assigned, locked, already labeled `stale`/`autoclose`, or have 10+ upvotes. A skipped issue doesn't change, so it stays at the front of the list and takes a slot again on every run. Over time those issues fill most of the window.

The window right after the Nov 6 22:06 UTC run, rebuilt from a snapshot of all 16,112 open issues:

| | Issues |
|---|---|
| Skip - 10+ upvotes | 599 |
| Skip - assigned | 186 |
| Skip - already stale | 155 |
| Skip - locked | 1 |
| Can label | 59 |

The last three runs labeled 69, 71 and 64 (Actions runs 42118507950, 42200120211, 42302084908). Meanwhile about 8,000 open issues have been quiet for 14+ days and meet every rule (GitHub search returns 8,000 for the query below), and about 237 more go quiet each day.

### What Should Happen?

Every issue the sweep reads should be one it is allowed to label, so the stale pass keeps up with the issues that go quiet each day.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Request `GET /repos/anthropics/claude-code/issues?state=open&sort=updated&direction=asc&per_page=100&page=1` through `page=10`, issues only (the job's token doesn't see pull requests, which matches the logged counts).
2. Count the issues that are assigned, locked, labeled `stale`/`autoclose`, or have 10+ thumbs-up reactions.
3. Compare the remainder with the `Done: N labeled stale` line in recent Issue Sweep runs.

### Suggested fix

Let search do the skipping, collect the results before labeling (labeling bumps `updated_at`, which would also shift later pages, the problem in #88548), and cap and pace the writes so a run stays under GitHub's limit on content-creating requests:

```diff
--- a/scripts/sweep.ts
+++ b/scripts/sweep.ts
@@ -40,6 +40,11 @@
   return response.json();
 }

+// GitHub allows about 500 content-creating requests an hour, and the closing
+// pass writes too, so label at most this many issues a run, a little apart.
+const MAX_STALE_LABELS_PER_RUN = 300;
+const WRITE_GAP_MS = 2000;
+
 // --

 async function markStale(owner: string, repo: string) {
@@ -51,39 +56,62 @@

   console.log(`\n=== marking stale (${staleDays}d inactive) ===`);

-  for (let page = 1; page <= 10; page++) {
-    const issues = await githubRequest<any[]>(
-      `/repos/${owner}/${repo}/issues?state=open&sort=updated&direction=asc&per_page=100&page=${page}`
+  // Search for only the issues this pass may label. Listing every open issue
+  // oldest-first filled the 10-page window with issues it always skips (10+
+  // upvotes, assigned, already stale), so each run labeled only a few dozen.
+  // reactions:< counts all reactions, so an issue with 10+ reactions of any
+  // kind is left alone; the exact upvote check below still applies.
+  const query = [
+    `repo:${owner}/${repo}`,
+    "is:issue",
+    "is:open",
+    "is:unlocked",
+    "no:assignee",
+    "-label:stale",
+    "-label:autoclose",
+    `updated:<${cutoff.toISOString().slice(0, 19)}+00:00`,
+    `reactions:<${STALE_UPVOTE_THRESHOLD}`,
+  ].join(" ");
+
+  // Collect the pages before labeling: labeling bumps an issue's updated
+  // time, which would shift later pages and skip issues.
+  const issues: any[] = [];
+  for (let page = 1; page <= 10 && issues.length < MAX_STALE_LABELS_PER_RUN; page++) {
+    const result = await githubRequest<{ items?: any[] }>(
+      `/search/issues?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}&sort=updated&order=asc&per_page=100&page=${page}`
     );
-    if (issues.length === 0) break;
+    if (!result.items?.length) break;
+    issues.push(...result.items);
+  }

-    for (const issue of issues) {
-      if (issue.pull_request) continue;
-      if (issue.locked) continue;
-      if (issue.assignees?.length > 0) continue;
+  for (const issue of issues) {
+    if (labeled >= MAX_STALE_LABELS_PER_RUN) break;
+    if (issue.pull_request) continue;
+    if (issue.locked) continue;
+    if (issue.assignees?.length > 0) continue;

-      const updatedAt = new Date(issue.updated_at);
-      if (updatedAt > cutoff) return labeled;
+    const updatedAt = new Date(issue.updated_at);
+    if (updatedAt > cutoff) continue;

-      const alreadyStale = issue.labels?.some(
-        (l: any) => l.name === "stale" || l.name === "autoclose"
-      );
-      if (alreadyStale) continue;
+    const alreadyStale = issue.labels?.some(
+      (l: any) => l.name === "stale" || l.name === "autoclose"
+    );
+    if (alreadyStale) continue;

-      const thumbsUp = issue.reactions?.["+1"] ?? 0;
-      if (thumbsUp >= STALE_UPVOTE_THRESHOLD) continue;
+    const thumbsUp = issue.reactions?.["+1"] ?? 0;
+    if (thumbsUp >= STALE_UPVOTE_THRESHOLD) continue;

-      const base = `/repos/${owner}/${repo}/issues/${issue.number}`;
+    const base = `/repos/${owner}/${repo}/issues/${issue.number}`;

-      if (DRY_RUN) {
-        const age = Math.floor((Date.now() - updatedAt.getTime()) / 86400000);
-        console.log(`#${issue.number}: would label stale (${age}d inactive) — ${issue.title}`);
-      } else {
-        await githubRequest(`${base}/labels`, "POST", { labels: ["stale"] });
-        console.log(`#${issue.number}: labeled stale — ${issue.title}`);
-      }
-      labeled++;
+    if (DRY_RUN) {
+      const age = Math.floor((Date.now() - updatedAt.getTime()) / 86400000);
+      console.log(`#${issue.number}: would label stale (${age}d inactive) — ${issue.title}`);
+    } else {
+      await githubRequest(`${base}/labels`, "POST", { labels: ["stale"] });
+      console.log(`#${issue.number}: labeled stale — ${issue.title}`);
+      await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, WRITE_GAP_MS));
     }
+    labeled++;
   }

   return labeled;
```

- `reactions:<10` counts all reaction kinds, so 5 issues with 10+ reactions but fewer than 10 upvotes would be left alone. The exact upvote check still runs on each result.
- At 300 a run, today's pile takes about three weeks. Two weeks later the closing pass will have more to close (two writes per close), so it needs a similar cap.
- I couldn't run the patched dry run live because my token hit search's secondary rate limit, so the counts come from the snapshot plus one live search count.

### Additional Information

Two related things worth deciding along with the fix:

- **A sweep that reaches everything will also close serious bugs.** Among closed issues that went through the stale label, 324 carry `data-loss`, 200 `reproduced` and 77 `high-priority` (for example #88605 and #84224). Adding `-label:data-loss -label:high-priority -label:reproduced` to the query, and the same skip in `closeExpired`, would keep those open. #93469 and #93791 (Windows `~/.claude` wiped, labeled `data-loss`) carry the stale label right now.
- **The popular issues in the window are mostly old bug reports.** 442 of the 599 carry `bug`, and 527 have been quiet since before September 1. In a 1,000-issue triage I ran, 26 of the 60 that landed in the sample were already fixed, duplicates, or too old to trust without a retest. Closing those would free up the window too.

The counts, the snapshot method and the triage data are in https://github.com/aarushkandukoori/claude-code-triage (see `sweep-fix/README.md` and `report/`).

## Environment

- Claude desktop app, Code tab, macOS (Claude Code 2.1.308 bundled)
- For comparison: Claude Code CLI 2.1.311 in a terminal

## What happens

An MCP server asks the user for confirmation through `elicitation/create`, a form with an empty schema. In the Code tab of the desktop app, no dialog appears. The server receives `{"action": "decline"}` about 0.5 s after the tool call starts.

The same server in Claude Code in a terminal shows the elicitation dialog, in every permission mode.

During `initialize`, the client advertises elicitation support to the server:

```json
{"roots":{"listChanged":true},"elicitation":{"form":{},"url":{}}}
```

Running Claude Code in stream-json mode, the way the desktop app drives it, shows that it forwards the request to the host:

```json
{"type":"control_request","request":{"subtype":"elicitation","mcp_server_name":"elicit-repro","message":"Do you want to continue?","mode":"form","requested_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{}}}}
```

So the request reaches the desktop app, which answers "decline" without showing anything.

## Expected

One of these:

1. The desktop app shows the elicitation form, as the terminal CLI does.
2. If it doesn't support elicitation, the client doesn't advertise the `elicitation` capability, so servers can fall back. Or it answers `cancel` rather than `decline`.

An automatic `decline` is indistinguishable from the user's own refusal. Servers and agents treat it as the user's answer, and the agent then tells the user they declined something they never saw.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Save this dependency-free server as `elicit-server.mjs`:

```js
import { createInterface } from "node:readline";

const send = (msg) => process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(msg) + "\n");
const pending = new Map();
let nextId = 1;

function ask(params) {
  const id = `srv-${nextId++}`;
  send({ jsonrpc: "2.0", id, method: "elicitation/create", params });
  return new Promise((resolve) => pending.set(id, resolve));
}

createInterface({ input: process.stdin }).on("line", async (line) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(line);
  if (msg.id !== undefined && pending.has(msg.id)) {
    pending.get(msg.id)(msg.result ?? { error: msg.error });
    pending.delete(msg.id);
    return;
  }
  if (msg.method === "initialize") {
    send({ jsonrpc: "2.0", id: msg.id, result: {
      protocolVersion: msg.params.protocolVersion,
      capabilities: { tools: {} },
      serverInfo: { name: "elicit-repro", version: "0.0.1" },
    } });
  } else if (msg.method === "tools/list") {
    send({ jsonrpc: "2.0", id: msg.id, result: { tools: [{
      name: "confirm_action",
      description: "Asks the user to confirm before doing something.",
      inputSchema: { type: "object", properties: {} },
    }] } });
  } else if (msg.method === "tools/call") {
    const started = Date.now();
    const answer = await ask({
      mode: "form",
      message: "Do you want to continue?",
      requestedSchema: { type: "object", properties: {} },
    });
    const text = `elicitation answer after ${Date.now() - started} ms: ${JSON.stringify(answer)}`;
    send({ jsonrpc: "2.0", id: msg.id, result: { content: [{ type: "text", text }] } });
  } else if (msg.id !== undefined) {
    send({ jsonrpc: "2.0", id: msg.id, result: {} });
  }
});
```

2. Register it: `claude mcp add -s user elicit-repro -- node /path/to/elicit-server.mjs`
3. In the desktop app's Code tab, ask: "Call the confirm_action tool once and report its raw result."
4. **Observed** (with a server that sends the same request): no dialog; the tool call returns `{"action":"decline"}` about 0.5 s after it starts.
5. In a terminal, run `claude` and ask the same thing. **Observed:** the elicitation dialog appears.

### Describe the bug

Following the examples in https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/copilot-cli-reference/cli-config-dir-reference#examples I'm trying to add "make fix" as command to be automatically allowed and it's not working, permission is always requested.

OTOH adding just "make" makes all make commands automatically allowed.

### Affected version

GitHub Copilot CLI 1.0.71.

### Steps to reproduce the behavior

1. Write this in `~/.copilot/permissions-config.json`
    ```json
    {
      "locations": {
        "/some/path": {
          "tool_approvals": [
            {
              "kind": "commands",
              "commandIdentifiers": [
                "make fix",
                "make:fix",
                "make(fix)",
                "shell(make fix)",
                "bash(make fix)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
    ```
2. `cd /some/path && copilot`
3. say "run make fix"

### Expected behavior

no questions asked

### Additional context

_No response_

I use remote MCP servers protected by Microsoft Entra ID (Teams, Outlook mail) through the Copilot SDK (`@github/copilot-sdk` 1.0.24, runtime 1.0.101), with `mcpOAuthTokenStorage: "persistent"` and the runtime's own sign-in (`mcp.oauth.login`); the host never handles OAuth tokens.

**What happens:** roughly 60–90 minutes after sign-in (the Entra access-token lifetime), tool calls to these servers start failing as unauthorized. `session.rpc.mcp.list()` keeps reporting them as `connected`. The agent then decides it cannot use the server and carries on without it. `rpc.mcp.restartServer({ name })` fixes it at once with no browser sign-in, so the stored refresh token is still valid.

**Reproduced locally** (no account involved): a local OAuth-protected MCP test server that issues 20-second access tokens, returns HTTP 401 on an expired token and supports refresh tokens. After the access token expires:
- every call fails with "MCP OAuth challenge required (HTTP 401)";
- `mcp.list()` stays `connected` for the whole time watched (60 s+);
- on runtime 1.0.101 the runtime makes no refresh request of its own and never recovers; on runtime 1.0.103 it sends a `needs-auth` status event and retries refresh grants, but calls still keep failing;
- `restartServer` makes the next call succeed.

**Expected:** like the VS Code MCP client, refresh the access token with the stored refresh token on a 401 (or shortly before expiry), retry the call once, and keep the server usable. At minimum, report the server as `needs-auth`/failed in `mcp.list()` instead of `connected` once its token is rejected.

Note: registering `onMcpAuthRequest` and answering `{ kind: "runtimeRefresh" }` for reason `refresh` did not fix calls either in my test, and it changes the behaviour for `initial` requests (a stored sign-in no longer reconnects at start when `initial` is cancelled), so it is not a host-side workaround.

Environment: macOS 26 and Linux; SDK 1.0.24 / runtime 1.0.101, also checked on SDK 1.0.26 / runtime 1.0.103.

