Related: #166289

## What Problem This Solves

Plugin authors who subscribe to diagnostic events from `register()` get every event twice while a separate discovery instance of their plugin is alive. The SDK runtime docs explain only that subscriptions are released at disposal, so nothing on that page tells them to gate the subscription. In #166289 a cost and token exporter double-counted between 3% and 24% of `model.usage` events per day because of this. The reporter asked for a note where diagnostic subscribers would see it.

## User Impact

Plugin authors reading the instance lifecycle section of `docs/plugins/sdk-runtime.md` now learn three things: diagnostic dispatch is process-wide, a live discovery instance receives the same events (same `seq`), and they should subscribe only when `api.registrationMode === "full"` or from a service `start()`. No runtime change.

## Why This Change Was Made

#172052 made diagnostic subscriptions belong to their plugin instance and released them at disposal, and its paragraph documents that. A non-borrowing `loadPluginRegistryHandle` caches its discovery instance, though, and until that instance is disposed its listener receives every dispatch. `registration-mode.md` tells discovery loads to skip sockets, workers, clients, and services, and it rules out listeners at module top level. It does not cover a diagnostic subscription made inside `register()`, and it does not say that the cost is double counting rather than wasted work. The new paragraph sits next to the ownership paragraph and links to the registration-mode page instead of repeating it.

## Evidence

I ran a temporary probe test on current `main` (`57382781c4c`, Node 24.22.1) through the real loader and diagnostics modules. The fixture plugin has a module-level `activeRegistration` guard and an `onInternalDiagnosticEvent` subscription in `register()`, as in the reporter's plugin. After each step one trusted `model.usage` event is emitted:

```
--- ungated (subscribes in every mode)
control: gateway load              registers=[full@m1]              deliveries=1 seqs=[1]
borrowing handle                   registers=[full@m1]              deliveries=1 seqs=[2]
non-borrowing handle #1            registers=[full@m1,discovery@m2] deliveries=2 seqs=[3,3] instances=[1,2]
same cached handle: true
non-borrowing handle #2            registers=[full@m1,discovery@m2] deliveries=2 seqs=[4,4] instances=[1,2]
after disposing handle             registers=[full@m1,discovery@m2] deliveries=1 seqs=[5]
--- gated (subscribes only when registrationMode === "full")
control: gateway load              registers=[full@m1]              deliveries=1 seqs=[1]
borrowing handle                   registers=[full@m1]              deliveries=1 seqs=[2]
non-borrowing handle #1            registers=[full@m1,discovery@m2] deliveries=1 seqs=[3]
non-borrowing handle #2            registers=[full@m1,discovery@m2] deliveries=1 seqs=[4]
after disposing handle             registers=[full@m1,discovery@m2] deliveries=1 seqs=[5]
```

- The discovery instance (`m2`) receives the same dispatch with the same `seq`, and the module-level guard never fires. This is what the reporter saw in production (identical payload, 1 ms apart).
- Disposing the discovery instance brings delivery back to 1, which matches the existing ownership paragraph.
- With the `full` gate the count stays at 1 throughout. The reporter's production rate also went to 0% after they shipped this gate (comment in #166289).

Docs checks: `oxfmt --check docs/plugins/sdk-runtime.md` passes, `pnpm docs:check-mdx` passes, and `git diff --check` is clean. `pnpm docs:check-links:anchors` reports the same two failures with and without this change (`/tools/acp-agents-setup` duplicate ID, `releases/2026.10.1.md:849`), and none on this page. The new link target `/plugins/sdk-entrypoints/registration-mode#registration-mode` is already used by `docs/plugins/sdk-entrypoints.md`.

